@*
    The status line every screen reports through.

    Rendered whether or not there is anything to say, and that is the whole point. Every page used to
    wrap this in `@if (_message is not null)`, which meant the live region was created at the same
    moment as its content. A region that appears already full is not announced by most screen readers:
    the till's entire feedback model — "Added Cola 500ml", "Sale CT01-20260325-0007 recorded", "A
    reason is required to open the drawer without a sale" — was silent for anybody not watching the
    screen. The region has to exist first, empty, and then change.

    `polite` rather than `alert`, deliberately. An operator is mid-task with a customer in front of
    them, and an assertive interruption on every scanned item would talk over the thing they are
    trying to do. Errors are distinguished visually; the announcement waits its turn.
*@

<div class="pos-message @(IsError ? "pos-message--error" : "pos-message--info")"
     role="status"
     aria-live="polite">
    @Message
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>What to say, or null for nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Message { get; set; }

    /// <summary>True when the message reports a refusal rather than a success.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool IsError { get; set; }
}
